# PostHog analytics (optional — leave empty to disable)
# Get your key at: https://posthog.com/signup
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_...
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend (optional — uncomment the block for the provider this app uses).
# The template ships with no backend wired; auth is local-only until you add one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase — https://supabase.com/dashboard → Project Settings → API
# The publishable key (sb_publishable_...) is the current name for what older
# docs call the anon key. It is safe to ship in the client; Row Level Security,
# not key secrecy, is what protects your data.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Firebase (JS SDK path) — Firebase console → Project settings → Your apps
# Not needed for the React Native Firebase path, which reads google-services.json
# and GoogleService-Info.plist instead. Those two files are gitignored; supply
# them to EAS as file-type environment variables, not by committing them.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-app.firebaseapp.com
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Google sign-in — Firebase path only, and only if you ran add-social-auth.sh.
# Not used with Supabase: there the Google client ID and secret live in the
# Supabase dashboard and never enter the app.
#
# Firebase console → Project settings → Your apps → your **iOS** app → Client ID.
# If there is no iOS app listed, add one first (bundle ID from app.json) — that
# step is what creates the iOS OAuth client, and without it Firebase rejects the
# token with auth/invalid-credential.
#
# The matching reversed form (com.googleusercontent.apps.<id>) also has to be a
# CFBundleURLSchemes entry in app.json. It is derived from this value at runtime,
# so there is nothing else to set here — see docs/backends/firebase.md.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=123456789-abcdef.apps.googleusercontent.com

# The Web client ID is NOT needed by this recipe. It is only required if you
# later move to @react-native-google-signin/google-signin, which wants it as
# `webClientId`. Left here so you know which one to grab when you do.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev sign-in bypass (optional, and only useful once a backend is wired).
# Adds a "Skip Sign-In (Dev)" button to the login screen that signs in through
# the real auth path, so you don't retype credentials on every reload.
#
# Use a throwaway test account, never a real user's — EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are
# inlined into the JS bundle, so this password ships in any build where it is
# set. Never add these to the production EAS environment group. app.config.js
# strips them from builds cut off `main` or `release/*` as a backstop, but not
# setting them is the safe default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_BYPASS_EMAIL=dev@example.test
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_BYPASS_PASSWORD=
